<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Klaus Störtebeker</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1360–<a href="/years/1401">1401</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Klaus Stortebeker</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Klaus Störtebeker (c. 1360–1401) was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victual-brothers">Victual Brothers</a>, a band of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> who raided shipping in the Baltic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> around 1400. Captured by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> fleet off Heligoland in 1401, he was beheaded in Hamburg with many of his men. He became a folk hero in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and much of what is told of him is legend.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/klaus-stortebeker.jpg" alt="Klaus Störtebeker" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Élisabeth Daynès · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rekonstruierter_Schaedel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
